  "text": "In a controlled laboratory setting, five different subpopulations representing two closely related rodent species were examined to see if they differ in how much water they drink each day. The results showed clear differences in water consumption between one subpopulation of the first species and one subpopulation of the second species, as well as between another pair of subpopulations from the two species. No meaningful differences were found among subpopulations within the same species. Thus, the only consistent variation in drinking behavior occurs between the two species themselves, and this pattern matches the distinct geographic and habitat ranges each occupies. Because all animals were raised under identical conditions, the observed differences are likely inherited rather than environmental. Proper statistical analysis confirmed that these differences are statistically significant and not due to random chance."
